  "state": {
    "case_id": "8",
    "case_summary": "A 5-year-old male was admitted to Children Medical Center (CMC) with inflammation and swelling in his left mandible and wrist, with no history of trauma. He had a history of multiple pneumonia episodes starting at seven months old. Upon admission, laboratory results showed an erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) of 84 mm/h, a white blood cell (WBC) count of 12100/mm3 (61% neutrophils, 39% lymphocytes), hemoglobin level of 11.3 g/dl, and a thrombocyte count of 386000/mm3. C-reactive protein (CRP) was 20 mg/dl, leading to the initiation of cephalexin (150 mg/kg/day). Radiographic imaging revealed osteolytic lesions in the distal metaphase of the hand and maxillary bone. Given his history of recurrent infections and multifocal osteomyelitis, a bone biopsy was performed, and immune system function was assessed. The bone biopsy showed non-necrotizing granulomatoid lesions. The purified protein derivative reaction had an induration of 10 mm. Tests for HIV, hepatitis B surface antigen (HBs), rheumatoid factor, and brucella agglutination were negative. Serum IgG was 1650 mg/dl (normal: 441–1135 mg/dl), with IgM and IgA in the high normal range at 250 and 175 mg/dl, respectively. No defects were found in humoral and cellular immunity, so phagocytic cell function was tested using a nitroblue-tetrazolium (NBT) slide test. Based on hematological and immunological tests (NBT = 0), antibiotic therapy was changed from cephalexin to co-trimoxazole (20 mg/kg/day, iv) along with interferon-γ (50 microgram/m2 every other day). After two weeks of treatment, the patient's condition improved, and he was discharged with a prescription for oral cephalexin (100 mg/kg/day) and co-trimoxazole (10 mg/kg/day) as prophylaxis. Eight months later, the patient was readmitted with a tender mass in the right upper quadrant (RUQ), severe dyspnea, persistent cough, and chest and abdominal pain in the epigastric area that had started 10 days prior. He was placed on cephalexin (100 mg/kg/day). A computerized tomography (CT) scan of the chest and abdomen revealed a hypodense area in the liver, with adjacent involvement of the lower right ribs and soft tissue reaction, indicating rib osteomyelitis. Sonography-guided drainage of the hepatic abscess was performed, and a sample was sent to the Mycology Department at Tehran University of Medical Sciences.\n\nThe microscopic examination of clarified specimen with KOH 10% indicated the branched, septated and dichotomous mycelia. The remaining specimen was also cultured on Brain Heart Infusion agar (BHI), Sabouraud's dextrose agar (S) and Sabouraud's containing 0.005% chloramphenicol (Sc). The S and Sc culture media were incubated at 25°C and BHI at 37°C. The colonies grew rapidly, attaining the diameter of 5 cm within 5 days and their color was bluish green. Cellophane tape preparations and slide cultures demonstrated septated, branched and hyaline hyphae with rough-walled conidiophores and radiated conidial heads.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
